 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "SCARED Child Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "The SCARED Child items are summed to obtain a raw score for the total and each of the five subscales. Average scores are also presented to allow for a comparison between subscales. Higher scores indicate higher levels of anxiety.\n\nA total score of 25 or more may indicate the presence of an anxiety disorder.\nFive factor scores are presented with associated cut-offs:\n1. Somatic/Panic (Items 1, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 19, 22, 24, 27, 30, 34, 38): assesses physical symptoms associated with panic and anxiety. A score of 7 or above may indicate the presence of panic disorder or significant somatic symptoms.\n2. General Anxiety (Items 5, 7, 14, 21, 23, 28, 33, 35, 37): evaluates excessive worry and anxiety that is not specific to particular situations. A score of 9 or above may indicate the presence of generalised anxiety disorder.\n3. Separation Anxiety (Items 4, 8, 13, 16, 20, 25, 29, 31): measures anxiety related to separation from attachment figures (typically parents/carers). A score of 5 or above may indicate separation anxiety disorder.\n4. Social Phobia (Items 3, 10, 26, 32, 39, 40, 41): evaluates fear and anxiety in social or performance situations. A score of 8 or above may indicate social anxiety disorder.\n5. School Avoidance (Items 2, 11, 17, 36): assesses anxiety and avoidance related to the school environment. A score of 3 or above may indicate significant school avoidance.\n\nTwo percentiles are presented to indicate how the SCARED Child Version scores compare to a group of children and adolescents with a diagnosed anxiety disorder and a community sample. A percentile of 50 compared to the Anxiety Sample is typical for children presenting with an anxiety disorder, which will correspond to a percentile compared to the Community Sample of approximately 95.\n\nOn first administration of the SCARED Child two plots are presented: (i) a plot showing the total and subscale percentiles compared to the anxiety sample so that the severity of each subscale can be compared, and (ii) an average score plot to enable a comparison between subscales. Upon multiple administration the progress of therapy can be tracked using two scales: (i) a plot showing the change in the subscale and total percentiles and (ii) the change in average scores.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "200197380b99fb09eda3fad253768e70e428f5c99190bebab807c6a043d9d24d",
    "pageNumber": 2,
    "text": "For comprehensive information on the SCARED Child, \nsee here\n.\nThe SCARED Child items are summed to obtain a raw score for the total and each of the five\nsubscales. Average scores are also presented to allow for a comparison between subscales.\nHigher scores indicate higher levels of anxiety. A total score of 25 or more may indicate the\npresence of an anxiety disorder.\nFive factor scores are presented with associated cut-offs:\n- Somatic/Panic (Items 1, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 19, 22, 24, 27, 30, 34, 38): assesses physical\nsymptoms associated with panic and anxiety. A score of 7 or above may indicate the presence\nof panic disorder or significant somatic symptoms.\n- General Anxiety (Items 5, 7, 14, 21, 23, 28, 33, 35, 37): evaluates excessive worry and anxiety\nthat is not specific to particular situations. A score of 9 or above may indicate the presence of\ngeneralised anxiety disorder.\n- Separation Anxiety (Items 4, 8, 13, 16, 20, 25, 29, 31): measures anxiety related to separation\nfrom attachment figures (typically parents/carers). A score of 5 or above may indicate separation\nanxiety disorder.\n- Social Phobia (Items 3, 10, 26, 32, 39, 40, 41): evaluates fear and anxiety in social or\nperformance situations. A score of 8 or above may indicate social anxiety disorder.\n- School Avoidance (Items 2, 11, 17, 36): assesses anxiety and avoidance related to the school\nenvironment. A score of 3 or above may indicate significant school avoidance.",
    "usage": "source_guidance_for_review_not_executable_scoring",
    "runtimeEnabled": false
   }
  ],
  "sourceRequirements": [
   "Five factor scores are presented with associated cut-offs:",
   "Two percentiles are presented to indicate how the SCARED Child Version scores compare to a group of children and adolescents with a diagnosed anxiety disorder and a community sample.",
   "A percentile of 50 compared to the Anxiety Sample is typical for children presenting with an anxiety disorder, which will correspond to a percentile compared to the Community Sample of approximately 95.",
   "On first administration of the SCARED Child two plots are presented: (i) a plot showing the total and subscale percentiles compared to the anxiety sample so that the severity of each subscale can be compared, and (ii) an average score plot to enable a comparison between subscales.",
   "Upon multiple administration the progress of therapy can be tracked using two scales: (i) a plot showing the change in the subscale and total percentiles and (ii) the change in average scores."
  ],
